WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.080
Let's examine how we currently handle
documents in AI.

2
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.080
The standard approach treats text as a
passive input, forcing

3
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.160
data through a fixed pipeline.

4
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.560
There is a mathematical mismatch here.

5
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.320
Real-world corpora, regulatory archives,
research databases, entire source code

6
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.040
repositories, are practically infinite.

7
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.160
The language models we feed them into are
finite.

8
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.200
The industry response has been to widen
the pipe.

9
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.480
We now have models boasting context
windows of a million,

10
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.320
even 2 million tokens.

11
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.640
But scale alone hits a wall.

12
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.360
Even at 2 million tokens, complex
synthesis across massive, unstructured

13
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.040
data sets breaks down.

14
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.320
Making the input pipe wider does not fix a
model's

15
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.640
structural inability to navigate and
process massive amounts of information

16
00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.280
effectively. Take the first common
workaround, context stuffing.

17
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.400
We load an entire massive document into
the model at

18
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.440
once. It reads linearly in a single pass.

19
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.200
As it moves deeper, attention degrades.

20
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.680
If new context changes earlier meanings,
it cannot reverse course

21
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:11.720
to reread. So engineers turn to the
alternative, retrieval augmented

22
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.720
generation, or RAG.

23
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.960
RAG bypasses the limit by carving
documents into pieces.

24
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:22.040
It retrieves pre-selected fragments and
injects them directly into the

25
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:26.120
prompt. By doing this, it destroys
structural context.

26
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:30.520
The model receives isolated shards of
data, completely disconnected from

27
00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.280
the surrounding architecture.

28
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.840
Cross-chunk coherence is lost.

29
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.400
You are forced into a losing trade-off.

30
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:41.480
You sacrifice structural coherence for
retrieval with RAG, or you

31
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.040
sacrifice attention detail for scale by
stuffing the context window.

32
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:50.680
In 2025, researchers published a paper
proposing a new pattern,

33
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.800
the Recursive Language Model, or RLM.

34
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:57.760
The RLM pattern abandons feeding a
document into a model.

35
00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.600
Instead, it treats the document as a
queryable environment.

36
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.880
The model uses tools to query data
iteratively.

37
00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.640
It scans, searches, pulls metadata, and
selectively targets specific sections

38
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.640
to read at full resolution.

39
00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.600
To put this theory into production, I
built RLM-MCP.

40
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.120
It operates as a model context protocol
server.

41
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.280
MCP provides a standardized framework that
exposes a lifecycle of

42
00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.120
tools to any compatible client, whether
that's clawed code or

43
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.120
cursor. The LLM is no longer a passive
reader trying

44
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.400
to parse an entire novel by flashlight in
one pass.

45
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.600
It operates as an active researcher with
the agency to

46
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.040
navigate the text.

47
00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.800
RLM-MCP organizes this navigation through
a five-step lifecycle.

48
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.120
Step one is session creation.

49
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.360
Everything the model does

50
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.120
is strictly scoped to an isolated session.

51
00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.280
Step two is document loading.

52
00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.600
The model can ingest inline text, files,
or directory globs.

53
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:07.480
To prevent redundancies, content is
evaluated by its SHA-256 hash.

54
00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.760
If the exact same document is loaded
twice, it resolves

55
00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:14.440
to a single cryptographic hash string,
preventing duplication.

56
00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:18.760
Moving to step three, on-demand dynamic
chunking.

57
00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.600
Standard RAG pipelines pre-chunk data
blindly.

58
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:26.920
Here, the LLM actively decides how to
slice text, choosing

59
00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.240
between fixed character counts, line
counts, or regex delimiters based

60
00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:34.280
on the exact task it is trying to execute.

61
00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.320
Step 4 is the search execution loop.

62
00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.840
The model runs literal regex or keyword
queries.

63
00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.640
The BM25 index is built lazily.

64
00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.320
It does not preemptively process the
corpus.

65
00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.080
Instead, the index generates in real time
across data blocks

66
00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.400
only when the first query is fired.

67
00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.880
Step 5 is artifact storage.

68
00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.720
Any output the model derives from the
document, summaries, extractions,

69
00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.440
or classifications is retained here.

70
00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.760
This modular lifecycle takes theoretically
infinite text and binds it

71
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.880
into explicit, manageable processing
operations.

72
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.240
Giving an LLM autonomous querying agency
introduces serious engineering risks.

73
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.920
Left unchecked, a model can spiral into
infinite search loops

74
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.480
and rapidly drain computational resources.

75
00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.800
To manage memory efficiently, the system
uses a cache staleness

76
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.440
fingerprint. It tracks document count,
content hashes, and the tokenizer

77
00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.280
version. If any of those parameters shift,
the index automatically

78
00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.680
rebuilds. We also implement strict
denial-of-service protections.

79
00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.160
RLM-M MCP establishes hard caps, budgets
of 500 tool calls,

80
00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:48.360
10,000 character document peaks, and a
50,000-character response maximum.

81
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.560
These caps prevent the model from
retrieving massive data blocks

82
00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.640
that overflow its context window.

83
00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:58.120
Concurrency semaphores cap batch
processing at 20 concurrent loads, while

84
00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.840
async locks ensure multi-agent safety.

85
00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.840
These strict constraints separate an
experimental academic pattern from a

86
00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:09.640
viable enterprise-grade server capable of
supporting a full engineering team.

87
00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:13.960
For enterprise AI, the baseline
requirement is eliminating untracked synthesis

88
00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:17.320
and hallucinations. You need to know
exactly where an answer

89
00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:21.400
came from. This brings us to structural
provenance, the primary

90
00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:25.480
architectural benefit of RLM over massive
context windows.

91
00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:29.400
Instead of offering an answer in
isolation, RLM maps every

92
00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.320
output back to its exact verifiable path
of derivation.

93
00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:37.440
Every artifact is recorded with exact
metadata, the literal character

94
00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.760
ranges, the unique prompt hash ID, and the
precise timestamp.

95
00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:46.560
Provenance shifts model outputs from being
probabilistic guesses into mathematically

96
00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.800
auditable and defensible artifacts.

97
00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:52.800
RLM is not a blanket replacement for every
LLM interaction.

98
00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.080
If your document fits comfortably within a
standard context window

99
00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.000
and you do not require rigorous provenance
tracking, standard insertion

100
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.520
remains the optimal path.

101
00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:02.960
You just paste it in.

102
00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:07.040
You deploy RLM when the corpus exceeds
reliable attention spans,

103
00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.400
or when your use case demands rigorous,
iterative, and defensible

104
00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.800
research. The RLM MCP architecture serves
as an operational bridge

105
00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:17.360
between infinite data and finite models.

106
00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.640
Moving beyond context windows requires
abandoning the idea of reading

107
00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:24.720
entire archives at once.

108
00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.280
Instead, we have to engineer environments
where models can actively

109
00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.920
search, synthesize, and prove their work.

